      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Frank Henenlotter's Basket Case epitomizes guerrilla filmmaking in early 1980s New York, shot for roughly $35,000 in genuine Times Square sleaze spots. This grotesque tale of conjoined twins - one normal, one deformed and carried in a wicker basket - perfectly captures the grimy exploitation era when NYC's 42nd Street grindhouses were still operating. The film shares DNA with <a href=\"/movie/22172\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"22172\">Street Trash</a> (1987) and <a href=\"/movie/23730\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"23730\">C.H.U.D.</a> (1984) in its authentic portrayal of pre-gentrification Manhattan's seedier side. The practical effects, though clearly low-budget, showcase impressive creativity that influenced later body horror films."
      },
      {
