      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The film's success spawned two sequels - <a href=\"/movie/27815\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"27815\">Basket Case 2</a> (1990) and <a href=\"/movie/27816\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"27816\">Basket Case 3: The Progeny</a> (1991) - though neither captured the gritty authenticity of the original. The practical effects work by Kevin Haney helped pioneer techniques later seen in films like <a href=\"/movie/22244\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"22244\">Society</a> (1989) and <a href=\"/movie/41428\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"41428\">Tetsuo: The Iron Man</a> (1989), proving low budgets could produce memorable creature features. Belial, the murderous deformed twin, became an icon of practical effects horror alongside contemporaries like the creatures from <a href=\"/movie/1091\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"1091\">The Thing</a> (1982)."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Beyond its horror elements, Basket Case offers a surprisingly poignant exploration of codependency and family trauma. This emotional core influenced later horror films like Deadgirl (2008) and <a href=\"/movie/10894\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"10894\">May</a> (2002) that blend psychological drama with body horror. The film's Times Square setting documents a vanished New York, much like Ms. 45 (1981) and <a href=\"/movie/27346\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"27346\">Maniac</a> (1980), preserving a crucial piece of exploitation cinema history. SUBHEAD: Legacy and Influence"
      },
      {
